: "min-saw-mon", "sourceName": "Min Saw Mon", "verb": "CREATES", "targetSlug": "mrauk-u", "targetName": "Mrauk-U", "context": "Min Saw Mon established Mrauk-U as the new capital city of his dynasty in 1430, strategically positioning it for defense and trade."}], "places": [{"name": "Mrauk-U, Myanmar", "role": "Capital City"}, {"name": "Launggyet, Myanmar", "role": "Former Capital"}, {"name": "Gaur, India", "role": "Exile Location"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Min Saw Mon", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "min-saw-mon"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q6862585", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Min_Saw_Mon"], "tags": ["Monarchs & Rulers", "political-systems", "empire-and-colonialism", "geopolitical-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Min Saw Mon's founding of the Mrauk-U Dynasty established a powerful kingdom that dominated the Bay of Bengal for over 350 years, creating a major trading hub and a unique cultural synthesis in Southeast Asia. His actions directly led to a period of sustained independence and prosperity for Arakan.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-18T23:26:33Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29665076093", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Naramate-hla Min Saw Mon, was the last king of the Launggyet Dynasty and the founder of the Mrauk-U Dynasty of Arakan, a former state in Myanmar (Burma).", "newValue": "Naramate-hla Min Saw Mon, the last king of the Launggyet Dynasty, fundamentally reshaped the political landscape of Arakan by founding the Mrauk-U Dynasty in 1430. After a quarter-century of exile, his return marked a pivotal moment, transitioning Arakan from a period of instability and fragmented rule to an era of consolidated power and burgeoning economic prosperity. His establishment of Mrauk-U as the new capital laid the groundwork for a formidable maritime kingdom that would dominate the Bay of Bengal for over three centuries, fostering a unique cultural synthesis at the crossroads of South and Southeast Asia.

Min Saw Mon's path to dynastic founder was forged through adversity. Driven from his throne at Launggyet in 1406 by the aggressive expansion of the Burmese Toungoo Dynasty, he spent 24 years in the court of the Bengali Sultanate in Gaur, cultivating a crucial alliance. In 1429, he returned to Arakan with substantial military backing from Sultan Jalaluddin Muhammad Shah, recapturing his ancestral lands and consolidating his authority. The strategic decision to establish a new capital at Mrauk-U in 1430, rather than rebuilding Launggyet, was a stroke of geopolitical genius, providing a defensible stronghold and an ideal port to leverage regional trade.

The Mrauk-U Dynasty, which Min Saw Mon inaugurated, evolved into a powerful and wealthy state, becoming a crucial entrepôt in the Indian Ocean trade network connecting India, Persia, Southeast Asia, and beyond. His reign initiated a distinct cultural flourishing, integrating indigenous Arakanese traditions with significant influences from Bengal, evident in architecture, coinage, and courtly customs. This legacy of independence, economic prowess, and cultural vibrancy persisted until the kingdom's eventual conquest by the Konbaung Dynasty in 1785."}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-18T23:26:33Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29665076093", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1380, Launggyet"}, {"title": "Dynastic succession and sovereign authority traditions", "type": "Idea", "year": "1380, Launggyet"}], "newValue": ["The ongoing political fragmentation and internal strife within Arakan created an opportunity for a unifying leader.", "The aggressive military expansion of the Burmese Toungoo Dynasty displaced Min Saw Mon, forcing his exile and driving his ambition for restoration.", "The strategic military and political support from the Bengali Sultanate provided the essential resources for Min Saw Mon's return and re-establishment of power."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-18T23:26:33Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29665076093", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Established dynastic legacies and territorial boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "1433"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "year": "1433"}], "newValue": ["The Mrauk-U Dynasty was established, becoming a dominant regional power in the Bay of Bengal for over 350 years.", "The city of Mrauk-U was founded as a major fortified capital and prospe